{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Auto Conventional Commit",
  "description": "After a Kiro turn completes, review accepted working-tree changes and create a Conventional Commits-compliant commit so SCM history stays aligned with Kiro's changes.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "agentStop"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "A Kiro turn just completed. Align source control with the changes that were just accepted by creating one or more Conventional Commits.\n\nFollow the conventional-commit skill workflow:\n1. Run `git status --porcelain` to see what changed. If there are no changes, do nothing and stop silently.\n2. Run `git diff` (unstaged) and `git diff --cached` (staged) to understand the actual content of the changes.\n3. Group related changes into logical commits. Prefer several small, coherent commits over one large mixed commit when the changes span unrelated concerns (e.g. steering docs vs. source code vs. scripts).\n4. For each logical group:\n   - Stage only the relevant files with `git add <specific paths>` (never a blind `git add .`).\n   - Commit with a Conventional Commits message: `type(scope): description` in imperative mood. Allowed types: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, build, ci, chore, revert. Add a short body when the change needs context, and a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer when applicable.\n   - For auditing, ALWAYS append an attribution footer as the last trailer block, separated from the body (and any BREAKING CHANGE footer) by one blank line. The footer MUST contain, first, a single `Assisted-by: Kiro` trailer, then one `Co-authored-by:` trailer for EACH distinct AI model that contributed since the last commit. Determine the contributing models from the session ledger (see the attribution step below). Emit one `Co-authored-by:` line per distinct model, ordered alphabetically by the trailer's name field. Use each model provider's no-reply address (e.g. Anthropic models -> <noreply@anthropic.com>, OpenAI models -> <noreply@openai.com>). Example shape for two models:\n       Assisted-by: Kiro\n       Co-authored-by: <Model A name/version> <noreply@providerA>\n       Co-authored-by: <Model B name/version> <noreply@providerB>\n     Pass the footer via a separate `-m` argument so the blank-line separation is preserved, e.g. `git commit -m \"type(scope): description\" -m \"<body>\" -m \"Assisted-by: Kiro\\nCo-authored-by: <model> <noreply@provider>\"`.\n5. Attribution ledger handling (do this BEFORE composing commit messages):\n   a. Record yourself as a backstop: determine your actual current model name/version (report the real model; never hardcode, assume, or invent a version) and append a single JSON line {\"ts\":\"<UTC ISO 8601>\",\"agent\":\"Kiro\",\"model\":\"<your real model>\",\"event\":\"agentStop\"} to BOTH `.kiro/session/model-usage.jsonl` (permanent) and `.kiro/session/pending-attribution.jsonl` (rolling), creating `.kiro/session/` and the files if missing.\n   b. Read `.kiro/session/pending-attribution.jsonl` and collect the set of DISTINCT `model` values. These are the models to list as `Co-authored-by:` trailers. If the file is missing or empty for some reason, fall back to your own current model as the sole co-author.\n   c. After ALL commits for this turn have succeeded, clear `.kiro/session/pending-attribution.jsonl` (truncate it to empty) so the next commit span starts fresh. Never clear or rewrite `.kiro/session/model-usage.jsonl`. If no commit was made (no changes), do NOT clear the pending ledger.\n6. Do NOT push. Do NOT use `--no-verify` (let prek hooks run). Do NOT amend existing commits.\n7. Flag—do not commit—any file that looks like it contains secrets (.env, credentials, private keys).\n8. After committing, print a one-line summary of each commit created, plus the list of models attributed.\n\nKeep the scope aligned with Cynosure's structure (e.g. steering, config, cli, topics, distribution, hooks, scripts)."
  }
}
